WEBVTT

00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:06.442
A campus needs cables a preparation schedule and
a delivery route These are three

00:00:06.442 --> 00:00:11.793
different questions Before choosing an algorithm
decide what the answer must describe

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.747
an entire network an order or one path

00:00:26.042 --> 00:00:31.572
Vertices are numbered from zero A line connects
two vertices an arrow

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:37.061
permits only its direction A weight gives the
cost Here original

00:00:37.061 --> 00:00:42.439
edge identifier one means the second input row
even after sorting

00:00:52.598 --> 00:00:57.843
An adjacency list gives each vertex its outgoing
connections An

00:00:57.843 --> 00:01:02.897
undirected edge needs entries at both ends with
the same original

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:08.345
identifier This representation lets us visit
neighbours without scanning the

00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:10.504
entire input each time

00:01:20.709 --> 00:01:26.497
Disjoint-set union tracks groups as connections
arrive Each vertex initially

00:01:26.497 --> 00:01:32.271
represents itself Parent points to the same
vertex size is one and there

00:01:32.271 --> 00:01:37.717
are five components Only a root stores the
authoritative size of its group

00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:44.864
Add the link zero to one Their roots differ so
two groups merge

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:50.518
Attach the smaller tree to the larger one on
equal sizes either

00:01:50.518 --> 00:01:56.035
consistent choice works Here root one joins root
zero Components

00:01:56.035 --> 00:01:59.225
becomes four and largest becomes two

00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:06.749
Next join two and three This creates a second
group of size two Now join one and

00:02:06.749 --> 00:02:12.208
three Find first follows each argument to its
root Roots zero and

00:02:12.251 --> 00:02:17.466
two have size two so merge them Four vertices
share a group

00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:19.790
vertex four remains separate

00:02:21.445 --> 00:02:27.097
Now add zero to two then four to itself Both
operations have equal

00:02:27.097 --> 00:02:32.736
endpoint roots Do not decrease components or add
sizes again Record a

00:02:32.736 --> 00:02:38.442
state after every operation including unchanged
states The last two

00:02:38.442 --> 00:02:41.864
answers are both two components largest four

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:49.328
Finding vertex three follows three two zero Zero
is the root

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:55.172
Path compression can redirect three straight to
zero The group still contains the

00:02:55.172 --> 00:03:00.743
same four vertices This parent link is internal
bookkeeping not a

00:03:00.743 --> 00:03:02.888
new cable in the input graph

00:03:04.625 --> 00:03:10.520
Try one compression step yourself The root is
already known Save the current parent

00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:16.691
redirect this vertex to the root then continue
through the saved link Pause for

00:03:16.691 --> 00:03:21.987
up to five minutes When ready compare your three
lines with this fragment

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:29.291
Balancing prevents avoidably tall trees while
compression shortens searches

00:03:29.605 --> 00:03:35.092
Rank is not group size With both heuristics the
total time for q operations

00:03:35.285 --> 00:03:40.921
is order n plus q times inverse Ackermann of n
amortized

00:03:41.249 --> 00:03:46.562
Output storage adds order q Ordinary DSU does
not support

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:48.000
arbitrary deletions

00:03:49.722 --> 00:03:55.402
The highlighted forest connects all five
buildings for total cost ten But delivery

00:03:55.402 --> 00:04:00.712
from zero to four uses zero two four for cost
eight These totals

00:04:00.728 --> 00:04:06.421
optimize different objects A spanning tree need
not preserve every shortest

00:04:06.421 --> 00:04:06.948
route

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:14.502
If vertex five is isolated no legal cable can
connect it It is a separate

00:04:14.502 --> 00:04:20.369
component A spanning forest on n vertices and c
components contains

00:04:20.413 --> 00:04:26.078
n minus c edges A tree also has no redundant
links reliability

00:04:26.078 --> 00:04:28.780
requirements need a richer network model

00:04:30.709 --> 00:04:36.157
Kruskal considers edges by increasing weight The
original identifiers remain

00:04:36.157 --> 00:04:41.359
attached to them Here the examination order is
two one six

00:04:41.456 --> 00:04:46.701
zero three five four Start a fresh disjoint-set

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:49.823
structure to represent the forest being built

00:04:51.528 --> 00:04:57.219
Edge two joins one and two for cost one Their
roots differ so accept it

00:04:57.534 --> 00:05:03.442
Edge one then connects zero to that group for
cost two Every accepted edge

00:05:03.471 --> 00:05:07.260
joins two trees instead of creating a cycle

00:05:08.862 --> 00:05:14.213
Edge six joins three and four for cost two We
now have two trees

00:05:14.541 --> 00:05:20.044
zero one two and three four A forest can contain
several

00:05:20.044 --> 00:05:25.411
growing pieces Its running cost is five and it
is still extendable to an

00:05:25.411 --> 00:05:26.033
optimum

00:05:27.612 --> 00:05:33.194
Should edge zero now be accepted Its endpoints
already have the path zero

00:05:33.318 --> 00:05:39.079
two one Adding zero to one would close a cycle
Skip it although

00:05:39.079 --> 00:05:44.473
its weight is smaller than the next accepted
edge Eligibility matters as well as

00:05:44.473 --> 00:05:45.381
price

00:05:47.028 --> 00:05:52.735
Edge three connects the two remaining groups for
cost five Total cost becomes

00:05:52.735 --> 00:05:58.523
ten The last two edges would create cycles The
answer has four original

00:05:58.523 --> 00:06:04.186
edge identifiers and one component On a
disconnected graph process

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:08.616
every component rather than assuming a single
tree exists

00:06:10.209 --> 00:06:15.886
A cut separates a current component from the
rest A lightest crossing edge is safe

00:06:15.956 --> 00:06:21.377
for some optimum containing the current forest
If an optimum lacks it an

00:06:21.377 --> 00:06:26.796
exchange along the resulting cycle replaces an
edge of no smaller weight

00:06:27.111 --> 00:06:30.057
Equal weights can allow different valid optima

00:06:31.709 --> 00:06:37.211
Prim solves the same objective with different
state Start at vertex zero Its

00:06:37.211 --> 00:06:42.741
boundary offers edge zero at cost four and edge
one at cost two Choose

00:06:42.741 --> 00:06:47.984
edge one to bring in vertex two A heap candidate
must retain destination

00:06:48.271 --> 00:06:51.706
weight and original edge identifier

00:06:54.875 --> 00:07:00.541
Vertex two exposes an edge to one costing one It
is now the cheapest

00:07:00.541 --> 00:07:06.449
boundary edge so take it The accepted weights
are two then one Prim

00:07:06.506 --> 00:07:12.212
is not scanning a globally sorted edge list its
available choices depend

00:07:12.212 --> 00:07:13.419
on the visited region

00:07:16.542 --> 00:07:22.208
The old offer from zero to one still exists in a
lazy queue but one is already

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:27.507
visited Discard that offer when removed Checking
only at insertion is not

00:07:27.507 --> 00:07:31.689
enough because the tree can change while an
entry waits in the queue

00:07:34.806 --> 00:07:40.526
The cheapest eligible offer now is edge three
reaching vertex three for

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:45.591
five Then edge six reaches four for two Running
totals are two

00:07:45.784 --> 00:07:51.572
three eight ten Kruskal and Prim agree on
optimum cost although

00:07:51.572 --> 00:07:53.920
their selection sequences differ

00:07:57.028 --> 00:08:02.667
When the queue is empty scan for another
unvisited vertex An isolated

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:08.401
vertex starts a new component and contributes no
edge A lazy heap may

00:08:08.401 --> 00:08:14.175
contain order m entries giving order n plus m
log of m

00:08:14.175 --> 00:08:17.678
plus one time and order n plus m memory

00:08:20.709 --> 00:08:26.239
Now use directed dependencies Depth-first search
follows one unfinished

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:31.578
branch then returns to work it suspended A
recursive call remembers both the

00:08:31.578 --> 00:08:37.353
vertex and where to resume its neighbour list An
explicit stack must preserve

00:08:37.353 --> 00:08:38.532
that progress too

00:08:42.612 --> 00:08:47.979
Unvisited means the search has not entered a
vertex Active means it is on the

00:08:47.979 --> 00:08:53.305
current depth-first path Finished means every
outgoing edge has been

00:08:53.305 --> 00:08:58.998
examined Here the active stack is zero one three
four A

00:08:58.998 --> 00:09:03.058
visited Boolean alone cannot express these
distinctions

00:09:07.125 --> 00:09:12.843
Vertex four has no outgoing work so finish it
and pop its frame Then

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.511
three finishes followed by one Zero still has
another neighbour Notice that

00:09:18.511 --> 00:09:24.204
finish records completion not discovery This
distinction is essential for

00:09:24.204 --> 00:09:27.612
both Kosaraju and topological ordering

00:09:31.625 --> 00:09:37.305
Enter two Its edge reaches three which has
already finished This is not a directed

00:09:37.305 --> 00:09:43.145
cycle Continue until two and zero finish For
this adjacency order

00:09:43.432 --> 00:09:49.220
the finish list is four three one two zero Other
traversal

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:52.194
orders may produce different valid lists

00:09:56.209 --> 00:10:01.861
At one hundred thousand vertices relying on a
small call stack can fail Store the

00:10:01.861 --> 00:10:07.619
next-neighbour index explicitly Advance it
before descending and finish

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:13.097
only when no neighbour remains Every vertex
enters and exits

00:10:13.097 --> 00:10:16.696
once each edge is inspected once

00:10:20.695 --> 00:10:26.347
In this graph zero one and two reach one another
Three and four

00:10:26.347 --> 00:10:32.135
form another mutually reachable group Vertex
five is isolated The

00:10:32.135 --> 00:10:37.853
arrow from two to three does not merge the
groups because there is no return path

00:10:40.209 --> 00:10:45.820
Kosaraju first runs depth-first search on the
original graph Cycles are

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:51.228
allowed here finish the pass rather than
rejecting them Use the displayed neighbour

00:10:51.228 --> 00:10:56.798
order Finishing from zero gives four three two
one zero Then

00:10:56.798 --> 00:11:02.505
isolated five finishes Include it even though it
was never reachable from

00:11:02.505 --> 00:11:03.291
zero

00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:11.359
Reverse every directed edge keeping vertex
identities fixed Clear the

00:11:11.359 --> 00:11:17.147
visited state before the second pass Use
decreasing first-pass finish order for

00:11:17.147 --> 00:11:22.813
the next seeds The second graph must be the
transpose of the first rather than a

00:11:22.813 --> 00:11:25.379
second traversal in the same direction

00:11:27.792 --> 00:11:33.594
Seed five finds only five Seed zero then
collects zero one and

00:11:33.594 --> 00:11:38.985
two in the transpose The later seed two is
already assigned Seed

00:11:39.069 --> 00:11:44.368
three collects three and four Skip assigned
vertices Colours name the

00:11:44.368 --> 00:11:49.638
groups numeric component labels need not match
this presentation order

00:11:52.056 --> 00:11:57.749
Think of each strongly connected group as one
vertex Their condensation has

00:11:57.749 --> 00:12:03.278
no directed cycle The remaining group with
greatest first-pass finish time has

00:12:03.278 --> 00:12:08.713
no outgoing path to another unassigned group in
the transpose The search stays

00:12:08.713 --> 00:12:11.496
inside that group and reaches all of it

00:12:13.848 --> 00:12:19.378
Return to the original directions Internal edges
disappear when groups are

00:12:19.378 --> 00:12:24.867
contracted The edge from two to three becomes A
to B If we add another

00:12:24.867 --> 00:12:30.207
original edge from one to three it produces the
same pair not a second

00:12:30.207 --> 00:12:34.294
condensation edge The isolated group still
exists

00:12:36.625 --> 00:12:41.979
Mutually dependent modules may need coordinated
changes Condensation

00:12:41.979 --> 00:12:47.561
exposes dependencies between groups but does not
fix their internal cycles

00:12:47.875 --> 00:12:53.498
Two passes the transpose and condensation need
linear work

00:12:53.935 --> 00:12:58.417
hash-based removal of duplicate pairs gives an
expected linear time bound

00:13:00.695 --> 00:13:06.429
Return to the acyclic task graph An arrow means
its source must happen before

00:13:06.429 --> 00:13:12.011
its target Reverse the DFS finish list to obtain
zero two one

00:13:12.162 --> 00:13:17.733
three four This order respects every dependency
Independent tasks

00:13:17.733 --> 00:13:23.125
can sometimes exchange places so a valid answer
need not be unique

00:13:30.625 --> 00:13:36.085
Build each vertex position in the proposed order
For edge one to three

00:13:36.278 --> 00:13:41.576
position one is two and position three is three
the inequality

00:13:41.576 --> 00:13:47.201
holds Check every edge and verify that every
vertex occurs

00:13:47.201 --> 00:13:52.854
exactly once This is useful for build
dependencies but does not optimize

00:13:52.854 --> 00:13:53.897
task duration

00:14:01.445 --> 00:14:07.122
Now add four to zero During the active path zero
one three

00:14:07.328 --> 00:14:12.817
four this edge returns to active zero No
complete order can respect that

00:14:12.817 --> 00:14:18.551
cycle Return acyclic false and an empty order A
cycle anywhere

00:14:18.551 --> 00:14:23.182
in the graph including a disconnected part
invalidates the answer

00:14:30.682 --> 00:14:36.331
A workpiece can take alternative processing
routes Vertices are stages reached

00:14:36.741 --> 00:14:42.502
arrows are allowed next transitions weights are
costs We seek the cheapest route

00:14:42.502 --> 00:14:48.288
from source zero These alternatives are not a
list of mandatory tasks that must

00:14:48.304 --> 00:14:49.456
all be performed

00:14:52.125 --> 00:14:57.938
Only the source cost is known initially Null
means no route has yet been discovered

00:14:58.253 --> 00:15:04.080
Process vertices in a topological order and
never relax from a null distance

00:15:04.408 --> 00:15:10.074
After processing zero costs to one and two are
five and two with original

00:15:10.074 --> 00:15:11.756
parent edges zero and one

00:15:14.472 --> 00:15:19.850
Without a discount processing one first offers
seven to vertex three

00:15:20.179 --> 00:15:25.845
Processing two improves that to six and offers
eleven to four Then three

00:15:25.845 --> 00:15:31.402
improves four to nine The route zero two three
four costs two

00:15:31.402 --> 00:15:34.049
plus four plus three which is nine

00:15:36.709 --> 00:15:42.291
Now change only edge one to three to minus four
representing a discount

00:15:42.646 --> 00:15:48.095
and restart the calculation Negative weights are
valid in a DAG After

00:15:48.095 --> 00:15:54.003
zero is processed again the cost to three is
still null We have not processed

00:15:54.304 --> 00:15:56.258
its incoming alternatives yet

00:15:58.862 --> 00:16:04.555
At vertex one the candidate is five plus minus
four giving one Three

00:16:04.555 --> 00:16:10.207
has no known cost so store one and original edge
identifier two The

00:16:10.207 --> 00:16:15.886
parent is an edge identifier not vertex one
Updating the number without its

00:16:15.886 --> 00:16:18.778
witness would leave an inconsistent result

00:16:21.459 --> 00:16:26.837
Vertex two later offers six to three Six is
worse than one

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:32.503
so retain the existing distance and parent It
also offers eleven to four

00:16:32.831 --> 00:16:38.522
Vertex three then offers four to vertex four The
final route

00:16:38.524 --> 00:16:44.418
is zero one three four with total five minus
four plus three

00:16:44.801 --> 00:16:45.655
equal to four

00:16:48.278 --> 00:16:53.863
An added isolated vertex five finishes with null
distance and parent

00:16:53.863 --> 00:16:59.270
minus one A finite distance can itself be minus
one Also reject a

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:04.825
directed cycle anywhere in the input even if
source zero cannot reach it

00:17:05.167 --> 00:17:08.099
That is a whole-graph requirement of this
assignment

00:17:10.695 --> 00:17:16.046
To reconstruct the route to four its parent edge
is identifier four

00:17:16.374 --> 00:17:21.562
three to four Move to three Parent edge two
leads from one to three

00:17:21.864 --> 00:17:27.665
Parent edge zero leads from zero to one Reverse
the collected vertices to obtain

00:17:27.665 --> 00:17:31.005
zero one three four

00:17:33.028 --> 00:17:38.923
Try the backward step yourself Original edge
four is the row three four

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:44.686
three Which column gives the previous vertex
Pause if needed It is column

00:17:44.686 --> 00:17:50.297
zero vertex three Column two gives the last edge
weight not the

00:17:50.297 --> 00:17:51.613
total route cost

00:17:53.695 --> 00:17:59.603
A correct sum is not enough Check forest
identifiers connectivity and

00:17:59.768 --> 00:18:05.489
edge count For components verify mutual
reachability and deduplicated

00:18:05.489 --> 00:18:10.758
condensation For an order check every edge
position For routes

00:18:10.937 --> 00:18:16.127
verify parent direction and total weight
Alternative valid witnesses should be

00:18:16.127 --> 00:18:16.926
accepted

00:18:18.625 --> 00:18:24.003
Include empty graphs where allowed isolates
repeated unions

00:18:24.155 --> 00:18:29.561
loops parallel edges negative weights and totals
beyond integer range

00:18:29.875 --> 00:18:35.337
Test deep chains and mixed requests in one
process Benchmark Kruskal and

00:18:35.337 --> 00:18:40.417
Prim on the same graphs three sizes at least
three runs

00:18:40.718 --> 00:18:44.887
median compilation excluded and documented
warm-up

00:18:46.639 --> 00:18:52.085
Here n counts vertices m input edges and q
unions

00:18:52.413 --> 00:18:57.873
DSU is amortized almost linear Kruskal and lazy
Prim

00:18:57.916 --> 00:19:03.471
include sorting or heap work Kosaraju
topological ordering

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:09.503
and DAG relaxation are linear hash deduplication
is expected linear

00:19:09.805 --> 00:19:13.974
A reconstructed route takes time proportional to
its length

